[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-15", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 15 — first valid row: 63.1 ops/s cross-host from a resident SUT; the SUT is not CPU-bound at its ceiling", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-15.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 15 - the first valid row: 63.1 ops/s cross-host from a resident SUT, and the SUT is not CPU-bound at its ceiling\n\n> Seed: iteration 14's S0 - a valid row needs a resident SUT; this host\n> stayed at 90-98% swap, so origin :cross-host on a fleet mac-mini.\n> Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2.\n\n## Generate\n\n  - The SUT reports its own residency (`/ldbc/meta :host-memory`: heap\n    used, RSS, swap; a swap-off host says 0%, not unknown) and binds to a\n    given host; the driver judges the swap refusal by the SUT's reading\n    when present. The cross-host row is judged by the machine that\n    serves. (First run refused on the DRIVER's swap because the SUT's nil\n    swap fell through - fixed and re-run.)\n  - SUT: simeon (murakumo fleet mac-mini, 10 cores, 16 GiB, swap off,\n    load 2-7), JDK 26, -Xmx8g, 8 threads, same 35k-post SF1 subset\n    (load+build 8 s there against 42 s on the paged host). Driver here,\n    over Tailscale (rtt ~14 ms; the LAN is not routed from this host).\n  - Prediction: with the SUT resident, per-op p50s return to the\n    in-process order (IC02/07 ~10 ms, IC08 tens of ms, IC09 ~200 ms) and\n    throughput at inflight 8 lands near 8 / mean-latency.\n\n## Rank\n\n    rate  sched  done  fail  on-time%  thru/s  delay-p95  empty  rows  ic02-p50  ic07-p50  ic08-p50  ic09-p50\n       2     40    40     0    100.00     2.0          1     22   6.3      30.1      31.5     128.1     228.3\n       4     80    80     0    100.00     4.0          1     47   5.8      16.5      23.0     112.3     217.0\n       8    160   160     0    100.00     8.0          1     83   7.0      15.4      14.4      94.7     188.3\n      16    320   320     0    100.00    15.9          1    139   8.2      14.1      13.1      80.9     181.8\n      32    640   640     0    100.00    31.7          1    281   8.1      15.1      12.6      91.4     201.0\n      64   1280  1280     0    100.00    63.1        333    568   8.1      16.5      14.8     130.5     295.7\n      96   1920  1920     0     12.29    63.5       9379    833   8.3      17.1      14.9     132.2     304.7\n\n    inflight 32, rate 96: 18% on-time, 69.6 ops/s, p50 ic02 345 / ic07 339 /\n    ic08 444 / ic09 642 -- the queue moved into the SUT, the ceiling did not move.\n    SUT JVM CPU at saturation: 284% (8 threads on 10 cores).\n\n    throughput 63.1 ops/s at rate 64   target 139,411 -> ratio 2.2e3 (raw)\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - Prediction held on shape: 100% on-time through 64, then a cliff; the\n    ceiling is 8 slots over a ~112 ms mean latency (Little: ~71/s).\n  - The number that matters for the next step is 284%: eight server\n    threads on a ten-core host, at the throughput ceiling, use under three\n    cores. The SUT is waiting on something that is not CPU - a lock, an\n    atom under contention, GC, or a lazily realised index shared across\n    threads. Whatever it is, more threads will not help until it is named.\n  - IC08 at 95-130 ms on a resident SUT is a real engine cost at this\n    subset (in-process on the 3,500-post subset: 1.9 ms non-empty, 17 ms\n    empty). 44% of answers are empty, and the empty IC08 was the slow one.\n  - 8.1x iteration 13 - but iteration 13 measured paging at a different\n    origin; the cross-host column starts at this row.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: BASELINE (first valid row, origin\n:cross-host). Growth is defined from here for this column.\n\nSeeds for iteration 16, in order:\n\n  - S4 (contention): at rate 64 / inflight 8 on simeon, take JVM thread\n    dumps (jcmd Thread.print) during the run and count where the 8\n    handler threads sit; predicted: most are BLOCKED/WAITING on one\n    monitor or a CAS retry rather than RUNNABLE in datalog. If so, the fix\n    is on the engine's shared state, and throughput should rise toward\n    8 threads x (1 / latency) with CPU following.\n  - S1' (planner): the empty IC08 is 9x the non-empty one in-process;\n    on this subset IC08 is the second-slowest op. Bound the person clause\n    first.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 with IC09's two-hop join bounded (simeon has\n    16 GiB; the full load fits, the warm-up did not finish).\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7.\n"}]
